1221-#  probe rather than against this comment. ***  The DECLINE rows R1 and R2 read the exit
1222-#  status on purpose -- `row("R2 cond says", ... , "55|exit0", ...)` -- and
1223:#  gdrom_cond_probe.py's own docstring gives the reason at its anchor `R1 and R2 assert
1224-#  announced AND survived`: a presence-only decline row cannot see exit(1) being
1225-#  reinstated, because fatal() PRINTS AND THEN THE PROCESS DIES.  Count 1, byte correct,
1226-#  row green, defect back.  So the rule is: an ACCEPT row may not rest on survival
1227-#  (guarded and unguarded code both survive); a DECLINE row MUST carry it (it is the only
1228-#  channel that distinguishes announce-and-continue from announce-and-die).
1229-#
1230-#  `R5 cond keep` is the KEYSTONE and it is ANCHORED: a fresh device has cond == 0, so
1231-#  the naive "write 0x55, read back 0" is satisfied by the correct fix AND by a mutant
1232-#  that zeroes cond on decline.  The row drives an ACCEPTED 0xef first, so the value it
1233-#  requires back is one only the accept arm can have placed there.
