 succeeded in 235ms:
1:#!/bin/bash
2:# GATE 9 -- every machine type AND EVERY SUBTYPE constructed under AddressSanitizer,
3:# three-way.
4:#
5:# This is the BREADTH gate. Everything else in this harness executes four machines; the
6:# fork changed 117 source files, 27 of them specific to architectures (alpha, ARM, i960,
7:# PowerPC, SGI) that no rig touches at all. This gate builds every machine type, attaches
8:# its devices, and lets ASan watch.
9:#
10:# It revives an instrument the project already used -- the CHANGELOG records a "run every
11:# machine under ASan sweep (23 machine types)" that found the macppc heap OOB (#23) -- but
12:# as a STANDING GATE rather than a one-off, and comparatively rather than absolutely.
13:#
14:# THE ASSERTION IS DIRECTIONAL, and that matters. The fork exists partly to FIX memory
15:# errors, so upstream being dirty where HEAD is clean is success, not failure. Only the
16:# reverse is a regression:
17:#
18:#     clean under pristine, dirty under HEAD   -> REGRESSION (this gate fails)
19:#     dirty under pristine, clean under HEAD   -> the fork doing its job
20:#     dirty under both                         -> pre-existing, reported not failed
21:#
22:# A MACHINE MUST BE HANDED A FILE OR IT NEVER CONSTRUCTS. Measured: `-E testmips` with no
23:# file prints usage and aborts before any device is attached -- a sweep built that way
24:# would test nothing and pass every time. With a dummy image the machine reports
25:# `model:`, `cpu:`, `memory:` and attaches devices. Machines with subtypes additionally
26:# need `-e <subtype>`; without it 15 of the 41 types never construct.  (This line said
27:# "13 of 37" until #444 re-measured it. The counts drift with the machine list; that is
28:# why nothing below asserts one.)
29:#
30:# ############################################################################
31:# #444 asansweepblind -- THREE MEASURED BLIND SPOTS, all of which left this gate GREEN
32:# while it was reporting things that were not true. Each fix below is annotated with the
33:# measurement that forced it; none of them is reasoned-from-source.
34:#
35:#  B1. IT COULD NOT SEE A CONSTRUCTION ABORT AT ALL.
36:#      sanhit() greps for ASan/UBSan text. A machine that calls abort() is not a
37:#      sanitizer hit, so it scored ZERO -- and the only construction assertion was a
38:#      FLOOR (`check_min "machine types constructed" 20`), which a handful of corpses
39:#      cannot breach.
40:#      MEASURED, by running the shipped gate against the same binaries this one uses:
41:#      **PASS (3 checks)** while 15 machine/subtype combinations were dying by SIGABRT.
42:#      It touched two of the fifteen (hpcmips/be-300, mvmeppc/mvme1600); both scored the
43:#      row `no  0  0` -- BYTE-IDENTICAL to the row a machine that merely wants a subtype
44:#      produces -- and both were absorbed by "machine types constructed 38 (>= 20)".
45:#      *** AND THE ABORT MESSAGE WAS NOT EVEN ON DISK. ***  core/interrupt.c:191-198 does
46:#      `printf("Aborting.\n"); abort();`, and abort() DOES NOT FLUSH STDIO. With stdout
47:#      redirected to a file glibc block-buffers it, so the whole diagnostic died with the
48:#      process: MEASURED, the log of `-E decstation -e 5400` was **0 bytes**. The gate then
49:#      read that empty file as `constructed=no, sanhit=0` -- INDISTINGUISHABLE from a
50:#      machine that merely needs a subtype. A dead machine and a fine one scored alike.
51:#      Fix: classify by EXIT STATUS (diedby(), below), which no buffering can lose, and
52:#      run under `stdbuf -o0 -e0` so the reason text survives for the human.
53:#
54:#  B2. IT SWEPT ONLY EACH TYPE'S *FIRST* SUBTYPE, so a defect in any later one was
55:#      invisible by construction. MEASURED: the shipped gate ran 14 subtypes in total,
56:#      one per type that needs one. Sweeping every subtype runs 58 and finds **15** dying
57:#      machines where a first-subtype-only sweep of the same 41 types finds **2** -- and
58:#      finds 4 ASan hits each on sgi/ip28, sgi/ip30 and sgi/ip35, which nothing in this
59:#      harness had ever executed.
60:#      The full sweep is 99 combinations and the whole gate -- both binaries, 198 launches
61:#      -- costs 55 s wall (measured), so there is no reason to bound it. It is not
62:#      bounded. If a future edit does bound it, the gate must SAY what it dropped -- do
63:#      not cap coverage silently.
64:#
65:#  B3. THE ALIAS PARSER SILENTLY RE-ATTRIBUTED ONE MACHINE'S SUBTYPES TO ANOTHER.
66:#      The old awk required the primary alias to match `[a-z0-9_.-]+`. **SGI's primary
67:#      alias is "silicon graphics" -- WITH A SPACE** (`gxemul -H`: `SGI ("silicon
68:#      graphics", "sgi")`), so the type line matched the line pattern, failed the alias
69:#      pattern, and fell through WITHOUT running `next` and WITHOUT updating `type`.
70:#      Its ten subtypes then attached to whatever type came before it in the help text,
71:#      which is `rpi`:
72:#          BEFORE:  S rpi ip12 .. S rpi ip35   (10 rows), `T sgi` absent, `S sgi *` = 0
73:#          AFTER :  T sgi, S sgi ip12 .. S sgi ip35 (10 rows), `S rpi *` = 0
74:#      Consequence measured, and it is worse than a wrong label: because no `T sgi` line
75:#      existed, THE TYPE LOOP NEVER RAN A SINGLE SGI MACHINE. Ten subtypes carrying a
76:#      real heap-buffer-overflow (arcbios.c:2391 set_env <- machine_setup_sgi) were not
77:#      swept at all, and no row anywhere was red.
78:#      Fix: firstalias() takes the first SHELL-SAFE alias out of the trailing
79:#      parenthesised group -- so `sgi`, not `silicon graphics`. And a line that yields NO
80:#      usable alias now emits an explicit `X`/`Y` marker that a check asserts to be zero,
81:#      so the next multi-word alias is RED instead of silent. THAT is the fix for the
82:#      class; renaming one machine would only have fixed the instance.
83:#      (Why not the true primary `silicon graphics`? Two reasons, both measured: the type
84:#      list is consumed by word-splitting shell loops, and `sgi` is what every other
85:#      record in this project calls it. `-E "silicon graphics"` does work -- verified --
86:#      it is just not a usable row key.)
87:# ############################################################################
88:set -u
89:HERE=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
90:. "$HERE/lib.sh"
91:
92:ASAN_HEAD=/tmp/gx-asan-head/gxemul
93:ASAN_PRI=/tmp/gx-asan-pristine/gxemul
94:DUMMY=$LOGDIR/dummy.elf
95:SHELLERR=$LOGDIR/asan_sweep_shellerr.txt
96:
97:gate_begin "asan-machine-sweep"
98:
99:[ -x "$ASAN_HEAD" ] || gate_skip "no ASan build -- see regress/README.md (build_asan)"
100:
101:# Leak reporting off: the emulator is killed mid-run, so exit-time leaks are expected and
102:# say nothing about correctness. ASan's memory-error detection is unaffected.
103:#
104:# verify_asan_link_order=0 is REQUIRED, not cosmetic. `stdbuf` works by LD_PRELOADing
105:# libstdbuf.so, which then precedes the ASan runtime in the initial library list; ASan
106:# refuses to start and prints "ASan runtime does not come first in initial library list".
107:# MEASURED both ways on decstation/5400: without the flag the run produced 155 bytes of
108:# that complaint and never entered main(); with it, 582 bytes of real emulator output
109:# ending in the abort diagnostic. Controls that the flag does not weaken detection:
110:# decstation/pmax still constructs (695 bytes, cpu:+memory:) and sgi/ip12 still reports
111:# its 4 sanitizer diagnostics.
112:#
113:# *** DO NOT TAKE "ASAN ACCEPTED THE FLAG" AS PROOF THE FLAG IS REAL. *** The discriminator
114:# this project uses for CLI effort tiers was tried here and FAILED to discriminate: this
115:# ASan silently ignores `no_such_asan_flag_xyz=0` and runs normally. The proof above is
116:# BEHAVIOURAL -- the flag changes what happens -- which is the only kind available.
117:export ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0:abort_on_error=0:print_summary=1:verify_asan_link_order=0
118:export UBSAN_OPTIONS=print_stacktrace=0
119:
120:# 15 machines abort here by design (see AB_EXEMPT). This host already has `ulimit -c 0`,
121:# but a host that does not would drop fifteen cores of a 93 MB ASan binary into $LOGDIR.
122:ulimit -c 0 2>/dev/null || true
123:
124:#############################################################################
125:#  AB_EXEMPT -- THE KNOWN-DEAD SET, DATED AND NAMED.
126:#
127:#  Same idiom as gate_offline.sh's SM_EXEMPT/SC_EXEMPT, and for the same reason: a gate
128:#  that carries debt must carry it OUT LOUD. Entries are `type/subtype:YYYY-MM-DD`.
129:#
130:#  WHY A LIST AND NOT A FLOOR. A floor cannot express "none of them died" -- that was
131:#  B1's second half. A hard count could, but it is brittle: it breaks every time the
132:#  machine list changes, for no defect. A NAMED list is neither. Adding a machine that
133:#  works changes nothing; a machine that starts dying is red the same day; and a machine
134:#  that stops dying is ALSO red (see the "no longer die" check), so the list cannot rot
135:#  into a lie the way SM_COVERED did before it was cross-checked against real work.
136:#
137:#  WHY THESE FIFTEEN, AND WHY THE DIRECTIONAL FRAME DOES NOT COVER THEM.
138:#  All fifteen were MEASURED on both binaries: they abort IDENTICALLY under pristine
139:#  39748e3 and under HEAD (rc=134, SIGABRT, same reason text). So the directional rule --
140:#  the one at the top of this file -- classifies every one of them as "pre-existing,
141:#  reported not failed", and a purely directional gate would print them and pass. That is
142:#  right for attributing blame and WRONG for knowing what is dead, which is why this gate
143:#  now asserts BOTH: `died on HEAD but constructs upstream` (directional, blame) and
144:#  `died and is not on this list` (absolute, inventory).
145:#
146:#  They are upstream's own unported-legacy markers, not fork damage. THREE shapes, each
147:#  read at the cited line rather than guessed, and the table's `reason` column prints
148:#  which one a given row hit:
149:#    * `fatal("TODO: ... rewrite\n"); abort();`  -- machines/machine_pmax.c:365,536;
150:#      machines/machine_sgi.c:170,218,226,238. The machine's interrupt wiring was never
151:#      ported to the current interrupt API and the port is commented out directly below
152:#      the abort. Hit by decstation/{3maxplus,maxine} and sgi/{ip20,ip22,ip24}.
153:#    * `interrupt_handler_lookup("...") failed. / Aborting.` -- core/interrupt.c:191-198,
154:#      reached from the same unported wiring via INTERRUPT_CONNECT (interrupt.h:77-80),
155:#      which DISCARDS the lookup's return value, so a miss cannot be handled by the caller.
156:#      This is the printf-then-abort() that B1 could not see.
157:#    * `bus_pci_add(): pci_data == NULL!` -- devices/bus_pci.c:220-222, a machine adding
158:#      PCI devices to a bus it never created. Hit by mvmeppc/mvme1600.
159:#
160:#  *** THE DATE IS MINE, NOT THE OWNER'S, AND IT WANTS CONFIRMING. ***  gate_offline.md's
161:#  own lesson is that "a deadline nobody chose is a deadline nobody owns"; the owner has
162:#  previously chosen tighter dates than proposed. 2026-11-30 is a placeholder chosen
163:#  because these are whole machine ports rather than one-line fixes -- it is not a
164:#  judgement that they may wait that long.
165:#
166:#  hpcmips/{be-300,e-105,agenda} WERE here, and their removal is this ratchet's first
167:#  discharge -- exactly as the previous version of this comment predicted ("when that
168:#  lands, the 'no longer die' check goes RED and the fix is to DELETE those three lines").
169:#  #444 (a29f87b) fixed their constructor deaths, but the check stayed GREEN for a further
170:#  day because this gate's instrumented binary was built on 2026-07-29 -- the July binary
171:#  still died, so the exemptions still "held".  *** THE RED ARRIVED ONLY WHEN #450's round
172:#  REBUILT the binary from current HEAD. ***  A ratchet graded against a stale artefact
173:#  ratchets nothing; that is `asanstale`, measured live, and the second time in one day the
174:#  same staleness converted a real result into a wrong one.
175:AB_EXEMPT="
176:    alpha/3000/300:2026-11-30
177:    decstation/3maxplus:2026-11-30
178:    decstation/maxine:2026-11-30
179:    decstation/5400:2026-11-30
180:    decstation/5500:2026-11-30
181:    mvmeppc/mvme1600:2026-11-30
182:    mvmeppc/mvme2100:2026-11-30
183:    sgi/ip19:2026-11-30
184:    sgi/ip20:2026-11-30
185:    sgi/ip22:2026-11-30
186:    sgi/ip24:2026-11-30
187:    sgi/ip27:2026-11-30
188:"
189:
190:#  A DELIBERATE COPY of gate_offline.sh's exempt_expired(). It belongs in lib.sh and this
191:#  round may not edit lib.sh, so the duplication is recorded rather than hidden -- see the
192:#  residual note at the end of this file. The behaviour it encodes was learned the hard
193:#  way there: an entry with NO date, an UNPADDED date, or a non-date never expired, and
194:#  still satisfied the coverage check. A malformed entry is RED and names itself.
195:ab_expired () {          # ab_expired <today> <entries...>
196:    local today="$1"; shift
197:    local out="" e d
198:    for e in "$@"; do
199:        case "$e" in
200:            *:*) ;;
201:            *) out="$out $e(NO-DATE)"; continue ;;
202:        esac
203:        d=${e##*:}
204:        case "$d" in
205:            [0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]) ;;
206:            *) out="$out ${e%%:*}(BAD-DATE:${d:-empty})"; continue ;;
207:        esac
208:        [ "$d" \< "$today" ] && out="$out ${e%%:*}"
209:    done
210:    printf '%s' "${out:-none}"
211:}
212:
213:ab_is_exempt() {         # ab_is_exempt <type/subtype>
214:    local e
215:    for e in $AB_EXEMPT; do [ "${e%%:*}" = "$1" ] && return 0; done
216:    return 1
217:}
218:
219:# Minimal file so machine construction proceeds past the loader.
220:printf '\x7fELF\x01\x02\x01\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x02\x00\x08\x00\x00\x00\x01' > "$DUMMY"
221:head -c 512 /dev/zero >> "$DUMMY"
222:
223:#  stdbuf is what makes an aborting machine's reason text reach the log at all (B1). The
224:#  assertions do not depend on it -- they are exit-status based -- but the human-readable
225:#  half of this gate does, so its absence is a row rather than a silent downgrade.
226:check "stdbuf present (abort diagnostics survive the abort)" \
227:      "$(command -v stdbuf >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo yes || echo no)" "yes"
228:
229:# Build the "type[ subtype]" work list from the emulator's own help text.
230:"$ASAN_HEAD" -H > "$LOGDIR/help.txt" 2>"$LOGDIR/help_err.txt"
231:
232:#  firstalias(): the first SHELL-SAFE alias inside the LAST parenthesised group on the
233:#  line. Both halves of that sentence are load-bearing:
234:#    * LAST group, because a machine's *name* may itself contain quotes and parentheses --
235:#      `Generic "bare" ARM machine ("barearm")`, `ARM-based "Android" machines
236:#      ("android-arm")`, `DECstation 3100 (PMAX) ("pmax", "3100", "2100")`. Harvesting
237:#      every quoted token on the line would pick up `bare` and `Android`.
238:#    * SHELL-SAFE, because the alias becomes a word in an unquoted `for` list and a row
239:#      key. `silicon graphics` is the primary alias for SGI and is unusable as either;
240:#      `sgi` is the next one and is what the rest of this project calls it.
241:#  An unusable line emits X (type) or Y (subtype) and is asserted to zero below, so the
242:#  NEXT machine with a space in its primary alias is red rather than mis-attributed. A
243:#  failed type line also sets type="?" so its subtypes cannot silently inherit the
244:#  PREVIOUS type -- that inheritance is precisely how SGI's ten became rpi's ten.
245:awk '
246:  function firstalias(line,   p,i,seg,n,arr) {
247:      p = 0
248:      for (i = length(line); i >= 1; i--) if (substr(line,i,1) == "(") { p = i; break }
249:      if (p == 0) return ""
250:      seg = substr(line, p)
251:      n = split(seg, arr, "\"")
252:      for (i = 2; i <= n; i += 2)
253:          if (arr[i] ~ /^[A-Za-z0-9_.+\/-]+$/) return arr[i]
254:      return ""
255:  }
256:  /^        [A-Za-z].*\("/ { a = firstalias($0)
257:        if (a != "") { type = a; print "T " a } else { type = "?"; print "X " $0 }
258:        next }
259:  /^            - .*\("/   { a = firstalias($0)
260:        if (a != "") print "S " type " " a; else print "Y " $0 }
261:' "$LOGDIR/help.txt" > "$LOGDIR/mtypes.txt"
262:
263:run_machine() {   # binary, type, subtype(may be empty), outfile  -> exit status of the run
264:    local bin=$1 t=$2 s=$3 out=$4 rc
265:    if [ -n "$s" ]; then
266:        timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 "$bin" -V -E "$t" -e "$s" "$DUMMY" </dev/null > "$out" 2>&1
267:    else
268:        timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 "$bin" -V -E "$t" "$DUMMY" </dev/null > "$out" 2>&1
269:    fi
270:    rc=$?
271:    return $rc
272:}
273:
274:# constructed? -- did devices actually attach
275:constructed() { grep -qa "cpu:" "$1" && grep -qa "memory:" "$1" && echo yes || echo no; }
276:# sanitizer hit? -- ASan or UBSan diagnostic present. These go to stderr, which is
277:# unbuffered, so unlike the emulator's own stdout they survive an abort.
278:sanhit() { grep -acE "AddressSanitizer|runtime error:|SEGV|heap-buffer-overflow|stack-buffer|use-after-free" "$1"; }
279:
280:#  diedby(): ONE DISTINGUISHABLE TOKEN per outcome, keyed on exit status only -- the same
281:#  shape as lib.sh's budget_class() and run_emu_progress()'s reason=. Exit status is the
282:#  one signal buffering cannot destroy, which is why the verdict rests on it and the
283:#  printed reason does not.
284:#    ok        exited under its own power. gxemul exits 1 on the dummy ELF ("incorrect
285:#              phentsize? 0, should be 32"), so 0 and 1 are both normal here.
286:#    abort     SIGABRT: an assert, or one of the `fatal(); abort();` legacy markers.
287:#    segv/...  killed by some other signal.
288:#    timeout   timeout(1) fired at 25 s. No machine here takes more than a second, so this
289:#              means a construction hang -- a defect, not load.
290:#    harness   *** NEVER EXEMPTIBLE. *** 125/126/127 are timeout(1)'s own failures: it
291:#              could not run, or the binary is missing or not executable. Scoring that as
292:#              a machine result would be the ABSENT class from run_emu_progress -- a
293:#              harness fault wearing a machine's name.
294:#    exitN     any other status. Unexpected from this binary, so it is a death too.
295:diedby() {
296:    case "$1" in
297:    0|1)         echo ok ;;
298:    124)         echo timeout ;;
299:    125|126|127) echo harness ;;
300:    132)         echo sigill ;;
301:    134)         echo abort ;;
302:    136)         echo sigfpe ;;
303:    139)         echo segv ;;
304:    *)           if [ "$1" -gt 128 ] 2>/dev/null; then echo "sig$(( $1 - 128 ))"
305:                 else echo "exit$1"; fi ;;
306:    esac
307:}
308:
309:#  The reason text for a death: the last two things the emulator managed to write.
310:#  Only meaningful because of stdbuf; if it is empty, say so rather than print nothing.
311:#
312:#  TWO FILTERS, both added after reading the first real output rather than guessing at it:
313:#   * drop `timeout: ...` -- that is timeout(1)'s own stderr ("the monitored command dumped
314:#     core"), which is about the harness, not the machine, and it crowded out the useful
315:#     line in every one of the fifteen deaths.
316:#   * do NOT drop indented lines. The first draft did (`grep -av '^ '`) and it threw away
317:#     exactly the line that names the cause: `    TODO: Legacy rewrite` is indented, so
318:#     four of the fifteen reported a bare `machine:` and said nothing at all.
319:death_reason() {
320:    local r
321:    r=$(grep -av '^timeout:' "$1" 2>/dev/null | grep -av '^[[:space:]]*$' \
322:        | tail -2 | sed 's/^[[:space:]]*//' | tr '\n' '|' | sed 's/|$//')
323:    printf '%s' "${r:-<no output -- diagnostic lost, check stdbuf>}"
324:}
325:
326:n_unparsed=$(grep -c '^[XY] ' "$LOGDIR/mtypes.txt")
327:n_types=$(grep -c '^T ' "$LOGDIR/mtypes.txt")
328:[ "$n_unparsed" != 0 ] && {
329:    note "machine lines in -H that yielded no usable alias:"
330:    grep '^[XY] ' "$LOGDIR/mtypes.txt" | sed 's/^/       /'
331:}
332:
333:n_total=0; n_built=0; n_regress=0; n_fixed=0; n_both=0
334:n_died=0; n_die_regress=0; n_harness=0
335:died_list=""; died_unexempt=""; built_types=""
336:: > "$LOGDIR/asan_findings.txt"
337:: > "$SHELLERR"
338:
339:#  THE WORK LIST: every type BARE (coverage of the no-`-e` path, which is what most of
340:#  this gate used to be) followed by every type/subtype pair. MEASURED on the 2026-07-29
341:#  ASan build: 41 types + 58 subtypes = 99 combinations, and the whole gate -- both
342:#  binaries, 198 emulator launches -- runs in 55 s wall. Nothing here is bounded or
343:#  sampled; if a future edit bounds it, print what was dropped.
344:{ grep '^T ' "$LOGDIR/mtypes.txt" | awk '{print $2" "}'
345:  grep '^S ' "$LOGDIR/mtypes.txt" | awk '{print $2" "$3}' ; } > "$LOGDIR/worklist.txt"
346:
347:printf "  %-14s %-16s %-9s %-8s %-8s %-8s %s\n" \
348:       "machine" "subtype" "built" "died" "pristine" "HEAD" "reason (if it died)"
349:while read -r t s; do
350:    key="$t/${s:--}"
351:    #  *** THE SHELL PRINTS "Aborted (core dumped)" FOR A SIGNAL-KILLED CHILD, ON ITS OWN
352:    #  STDERR, AT THE CALL SITE. ***  Measured: it is NOT suppressible from inside
353:    #  run_machine -- `( ... ) 2>/dev/null` around the command still leaks one line per
354:    #  death, because the shell doing the reporting is the one running the loop. Redirect
355:    #  it HERE, to a file, and report anything unexpected afterwards. Discarding it would
356:    #  hide a genuine harness error; leaving it interleaved makes fifteen deaths look like
357:    #  the gate itself crashing.
358:    run_machine "$ASAN_HEAD" "$t" "$s" "$LOGDIR/asan_head.txt" 2>>"$SHELLERR"
359:    rc=$?
360:    built=$(constructed "$LOGDIR/asan_head.txt")
361:    h=$(sanhit "$LOGDIR/asan_head.txt")
362:    d=$(diedby "$rc")
363:    reason=""; [ "$d" != ok ] && reason=$(death_reason "$LOGDIR/asan_head.txt")
364:
365:    p="-"; pd="-"
366:    if [ -x "$ASAN_PRI" ]; then
367:        run_machine "$ASAN_PRI" "$t" "$s" "$LOGDIR/asan_pri.txt" 2>>"$SHELLERR"
368:        prc=$?
369:        p=$(sanhit "$LOGDIR/asan_pri.txt")
370:        pd=$(diedby "$prc")
371:    fi
372:
373:    n_total=$((n_total+1))
374:    if [ "$built" = yes ]; then
375:        n_built=$((n_built+1))
376:        case " $built_types " in *" $t "*) ;; *) built_types="$built_types $t" ;; esac
377:    fi
378:    printf "  %-14s %-16s %-9s %-8s %-8s %-8s %s\n" \
379:           "$t" "${s:--}" "$built" "$d" "$p" "$h" "$(printf '%s' "$reason" | cut -c1-58)"
380:
381:    if [ "$d" = harness ]; then
382:        n_harness=$((n_harness+1))
383:        echo "HARNESS $key -- timeout(1) could not run the binary (rc=$rc)" >> "$LOGDIR/asan_findings.txt"
384:    elif [ "$d" != ok ]; then
385:        n_died=$((n_died+1)); died_list="$died_list $key"
386:        ab_is_exempt "$key" || died_unexempt="$died_unexempt $key($d)"
387:        echo "DIED $key -- $d -- $reason" >> "$LOGDIR/asan_findings.txt"
388:        #  Directional half: a machine upstream can construct and HEAD kills is the fork's
389:        #  doing, whatever the exemption list says.
390:        if [ "$pd" = ok ]; then
391:            n_die_regress=$((n_die_regress+1))
392:            echo "DEATH-REGRESSION $key -- ok upstream, $d on HEAD" >> "$LOGDIR/asan_findings.txt"
393:        fi
394:    fi
395:
396:    if [ "$p" != "-" ]; then
397:        if [ "$p" = 0 ] && [ "$h" != 0 ]; then
398:            n_regress=$((n_regress+1))
399:            echo "REGRESSION $key -- clean upstream, $h hit(s) on HEAD" >> "$LOGDIR/asan_findings.txt"
400:            grep -aE "AddressSanitizer|runtime error:" "$LOGDIR/asan_head.txt" | head -3 \
401:                | sed 's/^/       /'
402:        elif [ "$p" != 0 ] && [ "$h" = 0 ]; then
403:            n_fixed=$((n_fixed+1))
404:        elif [ "$p" != 0 ] && [ "$h" != 0 ]; then
405:            n_both=$((n_both+1))
406:        fi
407:    fi
408:done < "$LOGDIR/worklist.txt"
409:
410:#  Every enumerated type must construct in AT LEAST ONE of its forms. This is what
411:#  replaces `check_min "machine types constructed" 20`: that floor was satisfiable by 20
412:#  healthy types while 21 others were dead, and it is exactly what B1 walked through.
413:#  Per-combination construction is NOT assertable -- plenty of bare forms legitimately
414:#  refuse without `-e` -- but a type with no working form at all is a dead machine.
415:dead_types=""
416:for t in $(grep '^T ' "$LOGDIR/mtypes.txt" | awk '{print $2}'); do
417:    case " $built_types " in *" $t "*) ;; *) dead_types="$dead_types $t" ;; esac
418:done
419:
420:#  An AB_EXEMPT entry whose machine no longer dies is a lie in the inventory, and the
421:#  SM_COVERED lesson from gate_offline.sh is that a manifest nothing cross-checks verifies
422:#  the LEDGER, not the WORK. Deleting the line is a one-line fix and the message says so.
423:ab_stale=""
424:for e in $AB_EXEMPT; do
425:    k=${e%%:*}
426:    case " $died_list " in *" $k "*) ;; *) ab_stale="$ab_stale $k" ;; esac
427:done
428:
429:echo
430:note "machine types enumerated  : $n_types"
431:note "combinations swept        : $n_total  (every type bare, plus every subtype)"
432:note "of which constructed      : $n_built"
433:note "died (signal/timeout)     : $n_died  ->${died_list:- none}"
434:[ -x "$ASAN_PRI" ] && {
435:    note "fixed by the fork         : $n_fixed  (dirty upstream, clean on HEAD)"
436:    note "pre-existing, still dirty : $n_both   (reported, not failed)"
437:}
438:note "findings                  : $LOGDIR/asan_findings.txt"
439:#  ASan build provenance. NOT an assertion: a stale ASan tree is a build-freshness
440:#  question this gate cannot answer without rebuilding, and build_asan.sh is expensive.
441:#  It is printed because "HEAD" in the table means "HEAD as of this binary", and a reader
442:#  who does not know the date will over-read every green row in it.
443:note "ASan HEAD binary built    : $(date -r "$ASAN_HEAD" '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M' 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)"
444:[ -x "$ASAN_PRI" ] && \
445:note "ASan pristine binary built: $(date -r "$ASAN_PRI" '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M' 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)"
446:
447:#  Anything the shell wrote while reaping the sweep that is NOT the expected job-status
448:#  line for a death we already counted.
449:unexpected=$(grep -av 'Aborted\|Segmentation fault\|Killed\|Floating point\|Illegal instruction' \
450:             "$SHELLERR" 2>/dev/null | grep -av '^$' | head -5)
451:[ -n "$unexpected" ] && { note "unexpected shell diagnostics during the sweep:"
452:                          printf '%s\n' "$unexpected" | sed 's/^/       /'; }
453:
454:# ---- assertions ----------------------------------------------------------
455:#
456:#  *** FAILABILITY CONTROLS. ***  A gate nobody has watched fail is a gate nobody knows
457:#  works -- this harness's own "a green row means nothing" class. Eight mutants were BUILT
458:#  AND RUN against these binaries on 2026-08-21, each as a copy under /tmp (the tracked
459:#  file was checksummed before and after and never changed). Results, verbatim:
460:#
461:#    M0  the SHIPPED gate, unmodified          PASS (3 checks)  <- the defect, reproduced
462:#    M1  drop decstation/5400 from AB_EXEMPT   FAIL  "died and are NOT on AB_EXEMPT"
463:#    M2  put it back                           PASS (11 checks)
464:#    M3  list decstation/pmax, which lives     FAIL  "entries whose machine no longer dies"
465:#    M4  hpcmips/be-300 dated 2026-08-01       FAIL  "past its deadline OR malformed"
466:#    M5  hpcmips/be-300 with no date at all    FAIL  "... hpcmips/be-300(NO-DATE)"
467:#    M6  alias class reverted to [a-z0-9_.-]   FAIL  x2: unparsed=1 AND alpha/3000/300
468:#                                                    unbacked (the `/` in "3000/300")
469:#    M7  first-subtype-only sweeping           FAIL  x3: 56 swept; hpcmips/mvmeppc/sgi have
470:#                                                    no constructing form; 13 unbacked
471:#    M8  firstalias takes ONLY the primary     FAIL  x3: unparsed=1; ?/ip19..?/ip27 died
472:#        alias (the shipped B3 semantics)            unexempted; sgi/ip19..ip27 unbacked
473:#
474:#  M3 is the arm that fires when a machine gets FIXED, and M1 the arm that fires when one
475:#  starts dying, so the list has teeth in both directions. M8 is the one that matters most:
476:#  it is a ONE-TOKEN revert (`i <= n` -> `i <= 2`) and it does NOT merely re-lose SGI -- the
477:#  ten subtypes land under type `?` and are reported, where the shipped code silently filed
478:#  them under `rpi`. Losing a machine is now loud however the parse fails.
479:#
480:#  ENUMERATION IS COMPLETE. This is the B3 fix's teeth: it ties the row set to the
481:#  emulator's own help text instead of to a number, so it stays true when machines are
482:#  added or removed and goes red the moment a line stops parsing.
483:check "every -H machine line yielded a usable alias" "$n_unparsed" "0"
484:
485:#  Floors, kept only as the "a broken enumeration cannot pass by sweeping nothing" guard
486:#  the original comment wanted. They are deliberately slack: the check above is the one
487:#  that carries correctness. A count that PINS obstructs legitimate shrinkage -- that
488:#  mistake is recorded in gate_offline.sh -- so these are floors and stay floors.
489:check_min "machine types enumerated"      "$n_types" 40
490:check_min "combinations swept"            "$n_total" 90
491:
492:#  B1's replacement for `check_min "machine types constructed" 20`, in two halves.
493:check "every machine type constructs in at least one form" "${dead_types:-none}" "none"
494:check "machine/subtype combinations that died and are NOT on AB_EXEMPT" \
495:      "${died_unexempt:-none}" "none"
496:check "AB_EXEMPT entries whose machine no longer dies (delete the line)" \
497:      "${ab_stale:-none}" "none"
498:check "AB_EXEMPT: no entry past its deadline OR malformed" \
499:      "$(ab_expired "$(date +%Y-%m-%d)" $AB_EXEMPT)" "none"
500:check "harness faults (timeout(1) could not run the binary)" "$n_harness" "0"
501:
502:if [ -x "$ASAN_PRI" ]; then
503:    check "machines clean upstream but dirty on HEAD"      "$n_regress" "0"
504:    check "machines that construct upstream but die on HEAD" "$n_die_regress" "0"
505:else
506:    degrade "no ASan pristine build -- HEAD swept, but no three-way comparison"
507:fi
508:
509:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #446 sgi_eaddr
510:#  THE VALUE CLASS, WHICH THIS GATE'S OWN INSTRUMENT CANNOT SEE.
511:#
512:#  This gate owns "every machine and every subtype is constructed correctly", and ASan is
513:#  its instrument for one class of wrongness: memory safety.  #446 was exactly that class
514:#  -- machine_sgi.c handed arcbios_init() a 40-byte buffer it never initialised, and
515:#  set_env() -> strdup() read 41 bytes off the end on ip12/ip28/ip30/ip35.  ASan sees that,
516:#  and this sweep would catch its return.
517:#
518:#  *** WHAT ASan CANNOT SEE IS A BUFFER THAT IS TERMINATED AND WRONG. ***  `snprintf(buf,
519:#  1, ...)` writes only the NUL: no overflow, no report, and the guest is handed an EMPTY
520:#  MAC address.  `sizeof(eaddr_string)` is 8 on this host and yields "08:20:3".  Both are
521:#  silent here and both are real defects, so the sweep needs a second oracle of a different
522:#  kind rather than another ASan row.
523:#
524:#  This probe is that oracle.  It breaks on arcbios_init() in a real construction of the
525:#  five subtypes that reach it and requires the ethernet STRING to be the formatting of the
526:#  MAC BYTES passed alongside it -- a provenance check, so uninitialised garbage, an empty
527:#  string, a truncated string and a right-shaped wrong-octet string all fail.
528:#
529:#  IT RUNS THE NORMAL BINARY, NOT THE INSTRUMENTED ONE, and that is deliberate: the
530:#  property is about the value the code computes, which does not depend on the sanitizer,
531:#  and an ASan binary under gdb buys nothing here while costing startup time.
532:#
533:#  *** IT REPLACES A DETECTOR THAT WAS VACUOUS.  ***  The first version matched six regexes
534:#  over machine_sgi.c.  A pass-2 panel built SIXTEEN mutants and ALL SIXTEEN SCORED 7/7 --
535:#  among them `0*ETHERNET_STRING_MAXLEN` (two characters, zero compiler warnings, the full
536:#  overflow restored and ASan-measured), `#if 0`, a comment wrapper, and -- decisively -- a
537:#  217-BYTE FILE CONTAINING NOTHING BUT A C COMMENT.  Every one of those dies here.
538:EADDRLOG=$LOGDIR/sgi_eaddr.log
539:EADDRBIN=${GX:-$ROOT/build/gxemul}
540:if [ ! -f "$HERE/sgi_eaddr_probe.py" ]; then
541:    check "sgi_eaddr: probe present" "no" "yes"
542:elif [ ! -x "$EADDRBIN" ] || ! command -v gdb > /dev/null 2>&1; then
543:    #  degrade(), NOT gate_skip(), and the difference is load-bearing.  gate_skip() EXITS
544:    #  (lib.sh:137) -- calling it here would throw away the eleven checks this sweep has
545:    #  already recorded and report the whole gate as SKIP, which is worse than the gap it
546:    #  announces.  degrade() records the gap and still reaches gate_end.  Either way the
547:    #  missing coverage is NAMED: silent coverage loss scored green is the class this
548:    #  harness exists to refuse.
549:    degrade "sgi_eaddr: needs build/gxemul and gdb -- NOT run, so #446 is UNCOVERED here"
550:else
551:    python3 "$HERE/sgi_eaddr_probe.py" --binary "$EADDRBIN" > "$EADDRLOG" 2>&1 || true
552:    sed 's/^/       /' "$EADDRLOG"
553:    check "sgi_eaddr: the eaddr string is the formatting of its own MAC bytes" \
554:          "$(grep -c 'SGI_EADDR_PASS' "$EADDRLOG")" "1"
555:    check_min "sgi_eaddr: rows actually run" \
556:          "$(grep -cE '^  \[(ok|FAIL)\] ' "$EADDRLOG")" 10
557:fi
558:
559:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #450 arcbios shifts
560:#  TEN uncast `buf[3]<<24` sites in arcbios.c -- C99 6.5.7p4 UB whenever buf[3] >= 0x80,
561:#  and at three 64-bit-branch sites the sign-extension makes the assembled pointer's high
562:#  word one too small.  Fixed by width-matched casts at all ten (four sites already had
563:#  them: the author knew the idiom and applied it to 4 of 14).
564:#
565:#  *** WHY THE EXISTING THREE-WAY CANNOT PIN THIS. ***  sanhit() already counts
566:#  `runtime error:` lines, so these shifts fed the HEAD column above -- but the gate's
567:#  assertion is DIRECTIONAL, and reverting the casts lands on dirty-upstream/dirty-HEAD,
568:#  which is scored "pre-existing, reported but not failed".  A revert is INVISIBLE to it.
569:#  These two rows are the pin.
570:#
571:#  Row 1 is BEHAVIOURAL: the five SGI subtypes that reach arcbios construction must
572:#  produce ZERO left-shift lines (and zero ASan, which re-pins #446 through the same
573:#  fresh binary).  It inherits gate 9's staleness caveat -- the binary is built from git
574:#  HEAD by build_asan.sh, and nothing yet asserts its age (tracked as `asanstale`).
575:#  MEASURED while building this round: a sweep against the stale binary reported the
576:#  PRE-fix counts with rc=0, exactly the void that caveat names.
577:#
578:#  Row 2 is a STATIC census, deliberately binary-independent so it stays load-bearing
579:#  even when the instrumented binary is stale: every `buf[3] << 24` in the file must be
580:#  cast (14 casts, 0 uncast).  It is also the ONLY row that can see the six sites no
581:#  current image drives with buf[3] >= 0x80 -- the sweep cannot witness those, which two
582:#  pass-1 seats named as the reason a UBSan-only oracle is not enough.  And it catches
583:#  the mutant the sweep cannot: `(buf[3]&0x7f)<<24` kills the UB, silently drops bit 31,
584:#  and passes every sanitizer -- but drops the cast count to 13.
585:#  KNOWN LOUD FALSE-POSITIVE: a comment quoting the uncast idiom reddens the census.
586:#  That is the safe direction; #446's NUL-byte lesson says never quote mutant syntax
587:#  verbatim in comments anyway.
588:#  *** THE LIVENESS TERM IS LOAD-BEARING, added after a pass-2 seat named the hole: ***
589:#  zero matching lines from a run that never CONSTRUCTED the machine is not a clean run,
590:#  it is an absent measurement -- a binary that dies at startup would have scored green.
591:#  Each subtype must show its own model line, so "never ran" reads RED, not clean.
592:UB450=0
593:for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35; do
594:    timeout 60 "$ASAN_HEAD" -E sgi -e "$s" /dev/null > "$LOGDIR/ub450.txt" 2>&1
595:    sh450=$(grep -ac 'left shift of' "$LOGDIR/ub450.txt")
596:    as450=$(grep -ac 'ERROR: AddressSanitizer' "$LOGDIR/ub450.txt")
597:    live450=$(grep -ac 'model: SGI-' "$LOGDIR/ub450.txt")
598:    if [ "$sh450" != 0 ] || [ "$as450" != 0 ] || [ "$live450" = 0 ]; then
599:        UB450=$((UB450+1))
600:        note "#450 $s: shift=$sh450 asan=$as450 constructed=$live450 -- sites: $(grep -oE 'arcbios\.c:[0-9]+' "$LOGDIR/ub450.txt" | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')"
601:    fi
602:done
603:check "arcbios shifts: five reaching SGI subtypes construct AND give ZERO ubsan+asan lines" "$UB450" "0"
604:
605:#  *** THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS ROW WAS TWO GREPS AND A PASS-2 SEAT KILLED IT WITH ONE
606:#  TOKEN. ***  `(((uint32_t)buf[3]<<24) & 0x7fffffff)` keeps the cast SPELLING, passes
607:#  every sanitizer, and silently drops bit 31 -- both #450 rows stayed green over a real
608:#  value defect.  A pattern pins what its author thought of; the replacement freezes the
609:#  WHOLE statement (#444's S4 precedent) and exact-matches the multiset, so a mask insert,
610:#  a cast swap, a wrapper or a reorder reddens whether or not anyone predicted it.  The
611:#  probe strips comments and strings first, closing v1's loud false-positive too.
612:#  *** $HERE/../src, NOT $SEC: this gate never defines SEC, and the first committed
613:#  version of this line killed the WHOLE GATE at runtime with "SEC: unbound variable"
614:#  under set -u -- after the behavioural row had already printed ok, and with the wrapper
615:#  invocation still reporting rc=0.  A gate that dies between its rows and gate_end leaves
616:#  no verdict line, and rc=0 over a missing verdict is the killed-battery class in a
617:#  single gate.  bash -n cannot see an unbound variable; only a RUN can. ***
618:ARCSLOG=$LOGDIR/arcbios_shift.log
619:python3 "$HERE/arcbios_shift_probe.py" "$HERE/../src/promemul/arcbios.c" > "$ARCSLOG" 2>&1 || true
620:grep -E '^  (ok|FAIL) ' "$ARCSLOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
621:check "arcbios shifts: every assembly statement matches its frozen form" \
622:      "$(grep -c 'ARCSHIFT_PASS' "$ARCSLOG")" "1"
623:check_min "arcbios shifts: census rows actually run" \
624:      "$(grep -cE '^  (ok|FAIL) ' "$ARCSLOG")" 2
625:
626:#  RESIDUALS, recorded rather than fixed here (this round may edit only this file):
627:#   * ab_expired() is a verbatim-in-behaviour copy of gate_offline.sh's exempt_expired().
628:#     It belongs in lib.sh; two copies is exactly the "grep for its siblings" shape.
629:#   * Nothing in this harness asserts that /tmp/gx-asan-* is not months old. The note
630:#     above prints the date; no gate reads it.
631:#   * sanhit() compares NONZERO-ness, not magnitude, so a partial fix (measured: sgi/ip32
632:#     goes 5 hits upstream -> 2 on HEAD) reads as "pre-existing, still dirty".
633:gate_end
634:exit $?
1:#!/bin/bash
2:# GATE 7 -- three-way A/B against pristine 0.7.0 and the pre-batch fork.
3:#
4:#   pristine   39748e3   unmodified upstream GXemul 0.7.0
5:#   prebatch   2ffc91e   the fork immediately before the current batch
6:#   HEAD                 what is being shipped
7:#
8:# TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. Conflating them is the trap this gate exists to avoid:
9:#
10:#   vs PRISTINE  = CAPABILITY PRESERVATION. The fork carries ~290 deliberate corrections,
11:#                  so differences are EXPECTED and are not regressions. The only thing
12:#                  that counts against us is a machine that ran under upstream and no
13:#                  longer runs.
14:#
15:#   vs PREBATCH  = CHANGE-SET VERIFICATION. Everything not touched by the batch should be
16:#                  identical, and anything that moved must be attributable to a specific
17:#                  correction.
18:#
19:# `gate_ab.sh --build` (re)creates the two baselines first; without it they are reused.
20:#
21:# METHOD NOTES, both learned the hard way:
22:#
23:#  * Every run goes through run_emu_progress() (#419; run_emu() no longer has any caller),
24:#    which forces stdbuf -o0. On a pipe, gxemul's stdout
25:#    is 4 KB block-buffered and `timeout`'s SIGTERM discards a partial block -- so a guest
26:#    that produced 3 KB of good output scores ZERO. An earlier version of this comparison
27:#    reported a "capability regression" on luna88k that was entirely a lost buffer.
28:#  * Comparison is on SEMANTIC MARKERS, never on byte counts. Under a wall-clock timeout a
29:#    byte count measures how fast the host happened to be, not what the guest did.
30:set -u
31:HERE=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
32:. "$HERE/lib.sh"
33:
34:SEC=$ROOT/GXEMUL-SEC
35:PRISTINE_DIR=/tmp/gx-pristine
36:PREBATCH_DIR=/tmp/gx-prebatch
37:PRISTINE_REF=39748e3
38:PREBATCH_REF=2ffc91e
39:
40:build_baseline() {   # ref, dir, label
41:    local ref=$1 dir=$2 lab=$3
42:    echo "  building $lab ($ref) -> $dir"
43:    rm -rf "$dir"; mkdir -p "$dir"
44:    ( cd "$SEC" && git archive "$ref" ) | tar -x -C "$dir"
45:    # The repo does not track the exec bit (index mode 100644), so a tree produced by
46:    # `git archive` has a non-executable configure: it fails rc=126 and the generator
47:    # scripts die with make-level "Error 127". Note that greping the log for 'error:'
48:    # MISSES that, because make spells it "Error 127" -- the object count is the real tell.
49:    chmod +x "$dir/configure" 2>/dev/null
50:    find "$dir" -name '*.sh' -exec chmod +x {} + 2>/dev/null
51:    ( cd "$dir" && sh ./configure > configure.log 2>&1 && make -j12 > build.log 2>&1 )
52:    local objs; objs=$(find "$dir/src" -name '*.o' 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
53:    echo "    objects=$objs  binary=$([ -x "$dir/gxemul" ] && stat -c%s "$dir/gxemul" || echo NONE)"
54:    [ -x "$dir/gxemul" ] || { echo "    BUILD FAILED:"
55:        grep -E "Error [0-9]+|No rule|undefined reference" "$dir/build.log" | head -8; }
56:}
57:
58:if [ "${1:-}" = "--build" ]; then
59:    echo "## building baselines"
60:    build_baseline "$PRISTINE_REF" "$PRISTINE_DIR" pristine
61:    build_baseline "$PREBATCH_REF" "$PREBATCH_DIR" prebatch
62:    echo
63:fi
64:
65:gate_begin "a-b-baselines"
66:need_exec "$PRISTINE_DIR/gxemul" "$PREBATCH_DIR/gxemul" "$ROOT/build/gxemul"
67:
68:# probe <label> <binary> <logfile> <args...>  -> prints "marker=N ..." for the rig
69:# NOTE THE R: PREFIX. It opens the base image read-only and routes every guest write into
70:# a temporary overlay that is discarded when the run ends.
71:#
72:# Without it this function booted THREE DIFFERENT BUILDS SEQUENTIALLY AGAINST ONE SHARED,
73:# WRITABLE 2 GB IMAGE, so the runs were not independent: each build inherited whatever
74:# filesystem state the previous one left behind, including an unclean unmount when the
75:# 300 s timeout killed a guest that had reached its login prompt. The symptom was a
76:# non-deterministic FAIL -- HEAD came back 1:1:0 having passed 1:1:1 twice at the same
77:# commit with no code change. The image's mtime confirmed it: it tracked the most recent
78:# boot, not the download.
79:#
80:# Timing was ruled out separately by measurement: both builds reach `login:` in about
81:# 100 s against a 300 s budget, so this was never a marginal timeout.
82:LUNA_IMG="R:$IMAGES/liveimage-luna88k-raw-20250518.img"
83:
84:# #419: the 300 s wall budget above was replaced by a budget of GUEST WORK. See
85:# run_emu_progress() in lib.sh for the measurements. Three constants, each measured:
86:#
87:#   LUNA_BUDGET 12 G   Observed instructions-to-login spans 7.316-7.786 G over eleven boots.
88:#                      12 G is +54 % over the observed max. It bounds ONLY the failure path --
89:#                      a healthy boot stops at the MARKER long before it -- so over-provisioning
90:#                      costs nothing on the success path and removes per-host recalibration.
91:#                      8.5 G was rejected: it sits only ~5 % above the theoretical ceiling a
92:#                      maximally-precise usleep host would reach (~8.06 G).
93:#   LUNA_STALL  120 s  At the SLOWEST rate measured here (9.9 M instr/s -- 7,349,301,148
94:#                      instructions in 742 s, host saturated) one -N record arrives every
95:#                      3.4 s. That is a HANG, not a slow host -- but the claim is empirical,
96:#                      not absolute: this detector still reads `date +%s`, so a host that
97:#                      SUSPENDS or starves the process for two minutes looks identical.
98:#                      *** THE MULTIPLIER WAS OVERSTATED AS 35x. 3.4 s is a MEAN derived from
99:#                      total instructions over total wall time; the WORST observed gap is what
100:#                      matters, and measured directly on the pty path under saturation it is
101:#                      7.06 s (n=219: min 0.47, median 3.86, p90 5.54, p99 6.23). So the
102:#                      honest multiplier is 17x, not 35x. Still ample -- but a mean is not a
103:#                      worst case, and quoting one as the other is how a margin gets believed
104:#                      to be twice its real size. ***
105:#   LUNA_BACKSTOP 1800 s  2.4x the worst boot ever measured here (742 s, 8 busy loops on 8
106:#                      cores, which still reached login 1:1:1).
107:LUNA_BUDGET=12000000000
108:LUNA_STALL=120
109:LUNA_BACKSTOP=1800
110:
111:# *** THE CONSTANTS NEEDED THEIR OWN ROWS.  Measured in pass 2: multiplying LUNA_BUDGET by
112:# 1000, or LUNA_BACKSTOP by 4000, or disabling LUNA_STALL entirely, left ALL FOURTEEN CHECKS
113:# GREEN.  The selftest below exercises run_emu_progress() with LITERAL arguments, so it proves
114:# the function works while proving nothing about the values this gate actually passes it --
115:# the rows guard a different instance of the thing they appear to guard.
116:#
117:# These bounds are derived, not taste: the observed maximum instructions-to-login is
118:# 7,785,570,754, so a budget below ~1.05x that would false-FAIL a healthy boot and one above
119:# ~2x stops bounding anything.  The stall threshold must exceed several -N record intervals
120:# (~3.4 s at the slowest rate measured) and stay far below the backstop.  The backstop must
121:# exceed the worst boot measured (742 s) with margin.  A static check costs no boot. ***
122:LUNA_OBSERVED_MAX=7785570754
123:check_min "budget is above the observed max instructions-to-login" \
124:          "$LUNA_BUDGET" "$(( LUNA_OBSERVED_MAX * 105 / 100 ))"
125:check     "budget still bounds something (<= 2x observed max)" \
126:          "$([ "$LUNA_BUDGET" -le "$(( LUNA_OBSERVED_MAX * 2 ))" ] && echo yes || echo "no: $LUNA_BUDGET")" yes
127:check     "stall threshold is many -N intervals but well under the backstop" \
128:          "$([ "$LUNA_STALL" -ge 30 ] && [ "$LUNA_STALL" -le $(( LUNA_BACKSTOP / 4 )) ] && echo yes || echo "no: $LUNA_STALL")" yes
129:check     "backstop exceeds the worst boot measured (742 s) with margin" \
130:          "$([ "$LUNA_BACKSTOP" -ge 1200 ] && [ "$LUNA_BACKSTOP" -le 3600 ] && echo yes || echo "no: $LUNA_BACKSTOP")" yes
131:
132:# #419 SELFTEST. The classifier decides whether a row is a regression, a hang, a harness fault
133:# or merely a slow host, so it needs its own rows -- and they must NOT need the rig, or they
134:# would cost 15 minutes and never be run. Fake "emulators" drive every reason in seconds.
135:#
136:# WHY THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL: the BUDGET and STALLED branches are ONLY EVER ENTERED BY A FAILING
137:# RUN, because a healthy boot stops at MARKER long before either. So nothing on a green run
138:# exercises them, and a mutation that breaks them is invisible to every capability row here.
139:#
140:# *** AND THE HARDEST CASE IS THE ONE THAT LOOKS HEALTHY. Remove `-N` and the guest still
141:# boots, still prints every marker, and still classifies as MARKER -- there is nothing wrong
142:# with the boot. What is wrong is that the budget and stall protections were never armed, and
143:# the ONLY observable difference is that the instruction count stayed at zero. Pass 2 caught
144:# this surviving: the fake originally written for it never printed `login:`, so it had been
145:# built to match the guard rather than the threat. ***
146:selftest_progress() {
147:    local d=$LOGDIR/r419fake; mkdir -p "$d"
148:
149:    # Prints instruction records, then the marker. -> MARKER
150:    printf '#!/bin/sh\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo "[ $((i*100000000)) instrs; i/s=1 avg=1]"; sleep 0.2; done\necho "login: "\nsleep 30\n' > "$d/f_marker"
151:    # Prints ever-advancing records and never the marker. -> BUDGET (with a tiny budget)
152:    printf '#!/bin/sh\ni=1\nwhile :; do echo "[ $((i*100000000)) instrs; i/s=1 avg=1]"; i=$((i+1)); sleep 0.2; done\n' > "$d/f_budget"
153:    # One record, then silence while staying alive. -> STALLED
154:    printf '#!/bin/sh\necho "[ 100000000 instrs; i/s=1 avg=1]"\nsleep 60\n' > "$d/f_stall"
155:    # Prints two markers but NO instruction record, and never reaches the milestone. -> ABSENT
156:    printf '#!/bin/sh\necho "LUNA-88K BOOT"\necho "M88100"\nsleep 60\n' > "$d/f_absent"
157:    # *** THE ONE THAT MATTERS: a guest that BOOTS PERFECTLY with no instruction stream --
158:    # exactly what removing `-N` produces. It reaches the milestone, so `reason` is MARKER and
159:    # every reason-based check passes; only the instruction COUNT reveals that the budget and
160:    # stall protections were never armed. Pass 2 found this surviving; the fake above had been
161:    # written to match the guard rather than the threat, because it never printed `login:`. ***
162:    printf '#!/bin/sh\necho "LUNA-88K BOOT"\necho "M88100"\necho "login: "\nsleep 30\n' > "$d/f_nostream"
163:    chmod +x "$d"/f_*
164:
165:    local o r n
166:    o=$(run_emu_progress 20 500000000 5 'login:' "$d/o_marker.log" "$d/f_marker")
167:    r=${o#reason=}; r=${r%% *}
168:    check "progress selftest: marker stops the run" "$r" "MARKER"
169:
170:    o=$(run_emu_progress 20 500000000 5 'login:' "$d/o_budget.log" "$d/f_budget")
171:    r=${o#reason=}; r=${r%% *}
172:    check "progress selftest: instruction budget stops the run" "$r" "BUDGET"
173:
174:    o=$(run_emu_progress 20 500000000 3 'login:' "$d/o_stall.log" "$d/f_stall")
175:    r=${o#reason=}; r=${r%% *}
176:    check "progress selftest: a stalled guest is STALLED, not slow" "$r" "STALLED"
177:
178:    # If this row ever reads MARKER or BACKSTOP, a broken -N would score as an ordinary
179:    # timeout and the gate would go quietly green while measuring nothing.
180:    o=$(run_emu_progress 8 500000000 30 'login:' "$d/o_absent.log" "$d/f_absent")
181:    r=${o#reason=}; r=${r%% *}
182:    check "progress selftest: markers without an instruction stream are ABSENT" "$r" "ABSENT"
183:
184:    # A guest that BOOTS with no instruction stream is the dropped-`-N` shape, and its reason
185:    # is legitimately MARKER -- there is nothing wrong with the boot. The only signal that the
186:    # oracle was never armed is that the count stayed at zero, so THAT is what the real rows
187:    # assert. This row exists to keep the two facts nailed together: reason MARKER, count 0.
188:    o=$(run_emu_progress 20 500000000 10 'login:' "$d/o_nostream.log" "$d/f_nostream")
189:    r=${o#reason=}; r=${r%% *}
190:    n=${o#*ninstrs=}; n=${n%% *}
191:    check "progress selftest: a boot with no -N stream still reads MARKER" "$r" "MARKER"
192:    check "progress selftest: ...and is caught only by a zero instruction count" "$n" "0"
193:
194:    # A CRASH IS NOT A TIMEOUT. Measured on the previously shipped code: a fake emitting one
195:    # record then exiting 3 reported BACKSTOP -- putting a dead build into the single reason
196:    # a caller is allowed to excuse as host load. timeout(1) returns 124 if and only if IT
197:    # fired, so the exit status is what separates the two.
198:    printf '#!/bin/sh\necho "[ 100000000 instrs; i/s=1 avg=1]"\nexit 3\n' > "$d/f_crash"
199:    chmod +x "$d/f_crash"
200:    o=$(run_emu_progress 20 500000000 10 'login:' "$d/o_crash.log" "$d/f_crash")
201:    r=${o#reason=}; r=${r%% *}
202:    check "progress selftest: a crashed emulator is EXITED, not BACKSTOP" "$r" "EXITED"
203:}
204:selftest_progress
205:
206:# Sets R_REASON / R_INSTRS / R_MARKERS. Globals rather than a return value because the
207:# caller needs all three to score a row, and the reason is the whole point of #419.
208:luna_run() {   # binary, tag
209:    local log=$LOGDIR/ab_luna_$2.log out
210:    out=$(run_emu_progress "$LUNA_BACKSTOP" "$LUNA_BUDGET" "$LUNA_STALL" 'login:' \
211:              "$log" "$1" -N -e luna-88k -d "$LUNA_IMG" boot)
212:    R_REASON=${out#reason=}; R_REASON=${R_REASON%% *}
213:    R_INSTRS=${out#*ninstrs=}; R_INSTRS=${R_INSTRS%% *}
214:    R_MARKERS="$(count "$log" 'LUNA-88K BOOT'):$(count "$log" 'M88100'):$(count "$log" 'login:')"
215:    printf "       %-10s %-9s instrs=%-14s %s\n" "$2" "$R_REASON" "$R_INSTRS" "$R_MARKERS"
216:}
217:
218:if [ ! -f "$IMAGES/liveimage-luna88k-raw-20250518.img" ]; then
219:    degrade "luna88k image absent -- the only cross-build behavioural comparison did not run"
220:else
221:    note "luna88k (m88k): stop reason + 'LUNA-88K BOOT':'M88100':'login:' marker counts"
222:    luna_run "$PRISTINE_DIR/gxemul" pristine; PRI=$R_MARKERS; PRI_R=$R_REASON; PRI_N=$R_INSTRS
223:    luna_run "$PREBATCH_DIR/gxemul" prebatch; PRE=$R_MARKERS; PRE_R=$R_REASON; PRE_N=$R_INSTRS
224:    luna_run "$ROOT/build/gxemul"   head;     HD=$R_MARKERS;  HD_R=$R_REASON;  HD_N=$R_INSTRS
225:
226:    # #419 SCORING. The defect was that a load-induced timeout could not be told apart from a
227:    # capability regression. The naive cure -- calling every timeout INCONCLUSIVE -- was
228:    # MEASURED to be strictly worse: `degrade` and `gate_skip` both exit 77 -> SKIP ->
229:    # REGRESS_PASS_WITH_GAPS (exit 3), so a HEAD that hangs before login would turn today's
230:    # hard REGRESS_FAIL (exit 1) into a green-ish result. Green is what gets read. So the
231:    # reasons are scored individually and only ONE of them is ever inconclusive.
232:    #
233:    # ROW-SCOPED END CONDITIONS, and this is the subtle part: the signal that means "hung" for
234:    # HEAD is the EXPECTED state for pristine. Upstream 0.7.0 emits ZERO -N records on luna88k
235:    # (measured, five runs, 768-byte logs), so it can never complete an instruction budget --
236:    # scoring it by budget would turn a long-standing PASS into a permanent not-run.
237:    check "pristine 0.7.0 does not boot luna88k (expected)" "$PRI" "0:0:0"
238:    # #419 pass 2: assert the COUNT, not the reason. Scoring this by reason accepted STALLED
239:    # too, so "one instruction record followed by silence" would have passed a row whose name
240:    # claims there is no instruction stream at all. The row now says what it means.
241:    check "pristine emits no instruction records at all" "$PRI_N" "0"
242:    # PRI_R was assigned and never read after pass 2 removed its only consumer -- a seat
243:    # caught the dead variable. It is worth asserting rather than deleting: ABSENT proves
244:    # the no-stream path classified it, whereas BACKSTOP would mean the run sat for the full
245:    # 1800 s and nothing noticed.
246:    check "pristine is classified by the absent-stream path, not the backstop" "$PRI_R" "ABSENT"
247:
248:    # An absent stream from a build that is supposed to RUN is a harness fault -- the `-N`
249:    # argument or the extraction broke -- and a harness fault must be red, never inconclusive.
250:    #
251:    # *** #419 pass 2: THIS ASSERTS THE COUNT BECAUSE ASSERTING THE REASON DID NOT WORK.
252:    # `reason` is only ABSENT when the run did NOT reach the marker, so a build that boots
253:    # normally with `-N` removed classifies as MARKER and sailed straight through the previous
254:    # spelling of this check. Dropping `-N`, or breaking the extraction by one character, would
255:    # then leave every capability row green while the budget and stall protections this round
256:    # exists for were silently gone. A seat found it; the selftest had missed it because the
257:    # fake built for this case omitted `login:` -- it was written to match the guard rather
258:    # than the threat. ***
259:    for pair in "prebatch:$PRE_N" "HEAD:$HD_N"; do
260:        if [ "${pair#*:}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
261:            check "${pair%%:*}: -N instruction stream was actually observed" present present
262:        else
263:            check "${pair%%:*}: -N instruction stream was actually observed" "0 records" present
264:        fi
265:    done
266:
267:    # BUDGET means the guest was given a full allowance of WORK and still never booted. Host
268:    # load cannot produce that, so it is a real negative and stays a hard failure.
269:    # STALLED means the guest stopped executing entirely -- a hang, not a slow host.
270:    for pair in "prebatch:$PRE_R" "HEAD:$HD_R"; do
271:        case "${pair#*:}" in
272:        BUDGET|STALLED)
273:            check "${pair%%:*}: stopped for a reason load cannot cause" "${pair#*:}" MARKER ;;
274:        esac
275:    done
276:
277:    # The ONLY load-ambiguous outcome: wall clock expired while instructions were still
278:    # advancing. Even here it is inconclusive ONLY if prebatch was hit too -- gate_ab already
279:    # boots all three builds, so the load signal is free. If prebatch reached its marker the
280:    # host was demonstrably fine, and HEAD alone failing is a REGRESSION, not a casualty.
281:    if [ "$HD_R" = BACKSTOP ]; then
282:        if [ "$PRE_R" = MARKER ]; then
283:            check "HEAD hit the wall backstop while prebatch booted (regression, not load)" \
284:                  "BACKSTOP@$HD_N" "MARKER"
285:        else
286:            degrade "both HEAD and prebatch hit the ${LUNA_BACKSTOP}s backstop with instructions still advancing (HEAD $HD_N, prebatch $PRE_N) -- host too slow to decide; NOT a pass"
287:        fi
288:    fi
289:
290:    check "HEAD matches pre-batch on luna88k"               "$HD"  "$PRE"
291:    check "HEAD boots luna88k to a login prompt"            "$HD"  "1:1:1"
292:
293:    # Upstream 0.7.0 does NOT boot OpenBSD/luna88k -- measured, unbuffered: it emits its
294:    # banner -- 768 bytes of log, measured across five runs -- and no guest output whatsoever.
295:    # That is the expected baseline, so it is
296:    # asserted rather than treated as a failure. If it ever starts booting, the check above
297:    # fires and this note needs revisiting.
298:    #
299:    # #419 measured WHY, and it matters for the scoring above: pristine does not exit and does
300:    # not idle -- it SPINS, burning 100 % of one core, emitting fewer than 33,554,432
301:    # instructions in 300 s (< 111,848 instr/s). So "the process is still alive" carries no
302:    # information here, and an instruction budget could never complete: 12 G would take over
303:    # 29 hours. The stall detector catches it in LUNA_STALL seconds (120) instead -- a
304:    # prototype used 30 s and this note claimed that figure for months longer than it was
305:    # true; the shipped gate uses one constant for all three legs.
306:fi
307:echo
308:
309:# CAPABILITY PRESERVATION: every machine alias UPSTREAM advertises must still be
310:# advertised by HEAD.
311:#
312:# This replaces an alias check that could not fail. The old one harvested the alias list
313:# from HEAD's own -H and then grepped each machine's output for "Unknown machine" -- a
314:# string that appears NOWHERE in src/ (verified: 0 occurrences; the real message is
315:# "Unknown subtype", capitalised, so the lower-case pattern missed it too). `unknown` was
316:# therefore 0 for every possible input. Worse, harvesting from HEAD meant a dropped
317:# machine was absent from the list and never probed, so the one thing it claimed to detect
318:# was the one thing it structurally could not.
319:#
320:# Comparing the two advertised lists is a genuine assertion, needs no media, and is fast.
321:note "machine-alias capability: upstream's list must still be advertised by HEAD"
322:alias_list() { "$1" -H 2>&1 | grep -oE '"[a-z0-9_.-]+"' | tr -d '"' | sort -u; }
323:alias_list "$PRISTINE_DIR/gxemul" > "$LOGDIR/alias_pristine.txt"
324:alias_list "$ROOT/build/gxemul"   > "$LOGDIR/alias_head.txt"
325:n_pri=$(wc -l < "$LOGDIR/alias_pristine.txt")
326:n_head=$(wc -l < "$LOGDIR/alias_head.txt")
327:comm -23 "$LOGDIR/alias_pristine.txt" "$LOGDIR/alias_head.txt" > "$LOGDIR/alias_lost.txt"
328:comm -13 "$LOGDIR/alias_pristine.txt" "$LOGDIR/alias_head.txt" > "$LOGDIR/alias_gained.txt"
329:n_lost=$(wc -l < "$LOGDIR/alias_lost.txt")
330:n_gained=$(wc -l < "$LOGDIR/alias_gained.txt")
331:note "upstream advertises $n_pri, HEAD advertises $n_head"
332:[ "$n_gained" -gt 0 ] && note "added by the fork (expected, not a regression): $(tr '\n' ' ' < "$LOGDIR/alias_gained.txt")"
333:[ "$n_lost"   -gt 0 ] && note "MISSING FROM HEAD: $(tr '\n' ' ' < "$LOGDIR/alias_lost.txt")"
334:# A floor, so a broken -H or a broken regex cannot pass by yielding an empty list.
335:check_min "upstream alias list is non-trivial"   "$n_pri" 40
336:check     "machines upstream had that HEAD lost" "$n_lost" "0"
337:
338:gate_end
339:exit $?

