1429:#  *** A PYTHON DICT LITERAL ACCEPTS A DUPLICATE KEY IN SILENCE, AND THE LAST ONE WINS.
1430:#  MEASURED ON THIS FILE, HOURS AFTER IT WAS WRITTEN. ***
1431:#
1432:#  Two rounds added `sgi_eaddr_probe.py` to MANIFEST independently -- the author of this
1433:#  checker, and the #446 round whose probe it was.  Both landed, four lines apart, and
1434:#  `len(MANIFEST)` reported ONE entry.  Nothing complained, because nothing can: by the
1435:#  time the module object exists the earlier entry is already gone.  Here the two agreed
1436:#  on CLASS, so the behaviour was identical and the only damage was a stale line.  Had
1437:#  they disagreed -- one DETECTOR, one TOOL -- the file would have carried a visible,
1438:#  reviewed, *tracked* classification that the checker silently did not use, and the
1439:#  filename tooth that refuses `*_probe.py` in class `tool` would have been reading the
1440:#  wrong entry while looking correct in the diff.
1441:#
1442:#  It has to be caught by re-reading THIS FILE'S SOURCE TEXT, because the data structure
1443:#  cannot represent the fault it suffers from.  A duplicate is a SETUP FAULT, not a
1444:#  finding: it exits before any wiring is derived, so it can never be confused with a
1445:#  probe that is genuinely unwired.
1446:#  The open() is INLINE rather than a call to read() below: this guard runs at import time,
1447:#  fifty lines before read() is defined, and the first draft died with a NameError proving it.
1448:#  It failed loudly, which is luck -- a guard placed in a branch would have failed silently.
1449:def _refuse_duplicate_keys():
1450:    with open(os.path.abspath(__file__), "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
1451:        src = fh.read()
1452:    #  *** AN ANCHOR THAT NO LONGER MATCHES IS ITSELF A SETUP FAULT, NOT A REASON TO SKIP,
1453:    #  AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS GUARD DID `continue`. ***  A flagship review MEASURED
1454:    #  the consequence: `MANIFEST =  {` -- one extra space, valid Python, invisible to a
1455:    #  reader -- makes a literal find() miss, the guard skips, and a planted duplicate key
1456:    #  runs GREEN.  That is this file's own thesis turned on itself: a fault in a guard does
1457:    #  not fail loudly, it stops guarding.  The regex tolerates whitespace, and a miss is
1458:    #  now reported rather than shrugged at.
1459:    for name in ("MANIFEST", "EXEMPT"):
1460:        m = re.search(r"^%s\s*=\s*\{" % re.escape(name), src, re.M)
1461:        if m is None:
1462:            print("PROBEWIRING_SETUP_FAULT  the %s literal could not be located in this"
1463:                  " file's own source." % name)
1464:            print("  The duplicate-key guard cannot run, and a duplicate would pass"
1465:                  " UNSEEN.  Fix the anchor, do not delete the check.")
1466:            sys.exit(2)
1467:        i = m.start()
1468:        j = src.find("\n}", i)
1469:        seen, dupes = set(), []
1470:        for k in re.findall(r'^\s*"([^"]+)"\s*:', src[i:j], re.M):
1471:            (dupes.append(k) if k in seen else None)
1472:            seen.add(k)
1473:        if dupes:
1474:            print("PROBEWIRING_SETUP_FAULT  %s names the same key twice; a dict literal" % name)
1475:            print("  keeps only the LAST and says nothing.  Delete the stale line(s):")
1476:            for k in sorted(set(dupes)):
1477:                print("    %s" % k)
1478:            sys.exit(2)
1479:
2150:    return 0
